{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 8,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.319,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "ARITHMETIC PROGRESSIONS"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.873,
            0.105,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "57"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.141,
            0.903,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "Now, looking at the pattern formed above, can you find her monthly salary for the 6th year? The 15th year? And, assuming that she will still be working in the job, what about the monthly salary for the 25th year? You would calculate this by adding \\` 500 each time to the salary of the previous year to give the answer. Can we make this process shorter? Let us see. You may have already got some idea from the way we have obtained the salaries above."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.277,
            0.724,
            0.437
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} Salary for the  & 15th year  \\\\= &  Salary for the  14th year  + \\bar{\\bar{\\bar{\\bar{\\bar{\\xi}}}}} 500 \\\\= & \\bar{\\bar{\\bar{\\xi}}} \\left[ 8000 + \\frac{\\bar{5}00 + 500 + 500 + \\cdots + 500}{13  times } \\right] + \\bar{\\bar{\\xi}} 5000 \\\\= & \\bar{\\bar{\\xi}} \\left[ 8000 + 14 \\times 500 \\right] \\\\= & \\bar{\\bar{\\xi}} \\left[ 8000 + (1\\bar{\\bar{\\xi}} - 1) \\times 500 \\right] = \\bar{\\bar{\\xi}} 15000 \\quad  。  \\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.447,
            0.141,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "i.e.,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.283,
            0.447,
            0.703,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": "First solary  + (15 - 1) \\times  Annui increment:",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.477,
            0.757,
            0.496
          ],
          "content": "In the same way, her monthly salary for the 25th year would be"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.266,
            0.507,
            0.624,
            0.528
          ],
          "content": "\\left[ 8000 + (25 - 1) \\times 500 \\right] = \\bar{5} 20000",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.538,
            0.685,
            0.558
          ],
          "content": "{ \\mathrm { F i r s t ~ s a l a r y } } \\pm ( 2 5 - 1 ) \\times { \\mathrm { A n n u a l ~ i n c r e m e n t } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.567,
            0.9,
            0.609
          ],
          "content": "This example would have given you some idea about how to write the 15th term, or the 25th term, and more generally, the nth term of the AP."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.619,
            0.901,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": "Let $a _ { _ 1 } , \\; a _ { _ 2 } , \\; a _ { _ 3 } , \\; . \\; .$ . be an AP whose first term $a _ { \\mathrm { ~ i ~ } }$ is a and the common difference is d."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.668,
            0.159,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": "Then,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.221,
            0.693,
            0.664,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "the **second term** $a_{2}=a+d=a+(2-1)$ d"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.718,
            0.368,
            0.737
          ],
          "content": "the **third** term"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.396,
            0.718,
            0.89,
            0.738
          ],
          "content": "a_{3}=a_{2}+d=(a+d)+d=a+2d=a+(3-1)d",
          "image_source": "images/page_8_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.743,
            0.902,
            0.763
          ],
          "content": "the **fourth** term a = a + d = (a + 2d) + d = a + 3d = a + **(4 – 1)** d"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.967,
            0.563,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.147,
            0.819,
            0.834,
            0.838
          ],
          "content": "Looking at the pattern, we can say that the **nth** term $a_{_n} = a + (n - 1)   d.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.846,
            0.901,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "So, **the nth term** $a _ { n }$ **of the AP with first term a and common difference d is** given by $a _ { n } = a + ( n - 1 )$ **d.**"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
